# Guest and Provisional Account Containment

## Context

The 2026-08-20 access audit found that Skaia authenticates a new registration
immediately, assigns the `member` role, and issues a normal access/refresh token.
There is no account-age, email-verification, or TOTP-based mutation boundary.
The global DEFCON limiter is IP based (256 requests/minute for a new IP and
1,500/minute after IP graduation), while several domain and WebSocket mutation
paths have no durable per-user or per-action budget.

Immediate containment findings are:

- **Critical:** any WebSocket client, including a guest, can send
  `grengo:action`. When Grengo is configured, the backend forwards that payload
  through its control-plane client, including the backend-held passcode, without
  checking authentication, `admin.general`, a Grengo session, or step-up. Actions
  exposed by the frontend include compose down, ship, migrate, site removal,
  node/site export, arbitrary Grengo commands, and site creation.
- **Critical:** any unauthenticated WebSocket client can send `media:remove` and
  tombstone a persisted `media_history` row. The same guest path can add/remove
  queue items, advance or transition playback, and emit sound effects. Arbitrary
  presence routes also allocate retained in-memory media state and trigger a
  history query. Only pause/resume and clear-all currently check `home.manage`.
- **Critical:** any authenticated account can call `POST /api/store/wallet/topup`
  with an arbitrary positive amount and create its own credit transaction without
  payment-provider confirmation or an administrative policy.
- **High:** public `GET /api/pages/list` returns every active full page document
  without a visibility predicate, including private pages. It is unused by the
  frontend and bypasses the safe browse/preview paths.
- **High:** authenticated and guest WebSocket identities can overwrite their
  server-derived display name/avatar through `presence`; global chat then uses the
  spoofed values. Chat limiting is per connection, so reconnecting resets it.
- **High:** Grengo jobs, stats, storage, and hardware telemetry are broadcast to
  every connected socket, and any authenticated member can send `tp` to navigate
  another user because the handler does not enforce `presence.tp-here`.
- **High:** both analytics routes require only a valid JWT. A newly registered
  member can query arbitrary page/thread analytics and retrieve individual
  visitor rows containing IP address, user identity, and timestamps; there is no
  owner/editor/analytics permission policy.
- **High:** guests can invoke server-side datasource execution (including stored
  datasource environment data), enumerate async job IDs, mint guest LiveKit
  tokens where a route permits guests, submit checkout/order lookups, write page
  view analytics, and exercise all media actions. These exceptions have not been
  governed by one explicit guest-action registry.
- **High:** a fresh member can immediately create forum threads, comments, inbox
  conversations/messages, uploads, store orders/reviews/cart rows, likes, page
  interactive responses, and other account-owned records. Thread creation,
  inbox, uploads, orders, and interactive submissions have no new-account
  cooldown and no comprehensive per-action account budget.
- **Medium:** auth limiting is an in-process 10 requests/minute client/IP bucket;
  API-wide `RateLimitByIP` and `RateLimitByClient` middleware exist but are not
  mounted. Redis failure in DEFCON counting fails open. The IP can graduate to a
  shared trusted ceiling, so NAT peers and reconnects do not provide an account
  containment boundary.
- **Medium:** guest-safe user endpoints serialize the full `models.User`, including
  email and internal account-state fields, instead of a dedicated public profile
  DTO.

This plan supersedes the former smart-rate-limiter todo: its useful weighted
budgets, behavior isolation, telemetry, and admin-control goals are incorporated
here, but deterministic guest/account trust policy must precede adaptive rooms.

## Goal

- Make guests browse-only by default, with a small explicit exception registry
  for authentication/recovery and deliberately enabled global chat.
- Treat an account as `provisional` until 30 minutes after registration, unless
  it enables TOTP first; TOTP remains encouraged and optional.
- Permit provisional users only guest capabilities plus the minimum self-service
  routes needed to verify email, configure/enable TOTP, inspect their own trust
  state, refresh/logout, and recover the account.
- Enforce trust, authorization, rate, audit, and resource-cost policy before any
  database write or expensive backend work across HTTP and WebSocket transports.
- Preserve ordinary browsing and reviewed business exceptions without allowing
  unbounded or anonymous mutation.

## Non-Goals

- Require every user to enable TOTP.
- Treat IP address, Turnstile, email verification, JWT issuance time, or frontend
  state as sufficient account trust.
- Replace existing resource ownership, RBAC, sensitive-action step-up, validation,
  idempotency, storage quotas, or database constraints.
- Apply one low request ceiling to static assets or make shared NAT users inherit
  another user's account trust.
- Preserve a guest mutation merely because it already exists; every exception
  needs an owner, cost model, abuse controls, and tests.

## Access Contract

| Tier | Qualification | Allowed capabilities |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Guest | No validated account | Bounded public reads; register/login/refresh/verification/recovery; deliberately enabled global chat. No direct durable content/media mutations. |
| Provisional | Authenticated and `now < users.created_at + 30m`, with TOTP disabled | Guest capabilities plus own profile bootstrap, trust-status, email verification, TOTP setup/enable, logout, and necessary session recovery. |
| Established | Account age is at least 30 minutes, or TOTP is enabled | Normal authenticated actions, still subject to ownership/RBAC, action budgets, step-up, validation, and audit. |
| Privileged | Established plus required permission/power and fresh action-bound step-up | Sensitive or administrative actions only through centralized service policy. |

The backend computes the tier from persisted account creation time and current
TOTP state. Access-token `iat` is not account age. Existing accounts older than
30 minutes are established on rollout. Disabling TOTP does not make an older
account provisional; an account still inside its original 30-minute window
returns to provisional unless an explicit security policy says the disable
operation itself requires waiting. Clock comparisons use server UTC.

## Steps

1. **Contain confirmed critical paths before the broader rollout**
   - Remove `grengo:action` from ordinary client ingress until it requires an
     authenticated established administrator, `admin.general`, a live Grengo
     session, a fresh action/target-bound step-up grant, an action/argument
     allowlist, bounded concurrency, and an audit record. Never let a browser
     borrow the backend's standing control-plane passcode.
   - Deliver Grengo jobs/stats/storage/hardware/logs only through authorized
     subscriptions or direct admin sessions. Test guest, member, stale/absent
     session, missing permission, malformed action, and backend-passcode cases.
   - Enforce `presence.tp-here` and account trust before teleport; bind the source
     and target to canonical session identities and validate the destination.
   - Deny guest and provisional media mutation. Add a route-aware service policy
     for queue control, playback transitions, sound effects, individual history
     deletion, and clear-all; do not rely on the WebSocket message allowlist.
   - Apply the database mutation synchronously before changing/broadcasting media
     state, attribute lifecycle audit rows to the acting user, handle errors, and
     test guest, provisional, established non-manager, owner, and manager cases.
   - Bound and expire per-route media state; validate canonical route length and
     existence before loading history so arbitrary guest strings cannot retain
     map entries or force database queries.
   - Remove the wallet top-up route from production or require a verified payment
     provider callback/ledger transaction. Any development credit faucet must be
     build/environment gated, loopback-only, permission protected, audited, and
     impossible to enable accidentally in production.
   - Remove the unused `/api/pages/list` route or replace it with the authorized,
     content-free browse contract. Never post-filter private full documents.
   - Replace public user serialization with an explicit public-profile DTO that
     excludes email, suspension/security internals, permissions, and private URLs.
   - Put analytics summary and individual-visitor reads behind service-level
     resource-owner/editor or explicit analytics permission policy. Return only
     aggregates by default; raw IP/identity rows require a separately justified,
     stepped-up admin capability or should be removed from application APIs.

2. **Freeze a route/action capability inventory**
   - Generate a checked registry for every HTTP method/route and client-originated
     WebSocket message: read/write, authentication tier, resource policy, cost,
     rate key, persistence side effect, audit event, and failure behavior.
   - Classify browsing side effects such as page/thread views separately. Dedupe
     them in Redis by tenant + resource + guest session/account + time window,
     then persist asynchronously with a bounded queue; limiter/cache failure must
     not turn a public read into unbounded synchronous writes.
   - Review guest checkout as a business exception. If retained, require bounded
     idempotency, stock/payment transaction safety, a narrow order DTO, distributed
     IP/session budgets, and Turnstile only when risk warrants it. Otherwise require
     an established account. Public datasource execution, job lookup, LiveKit
     tokens, and media mutation default to denied until separately approved.
   - Add a source-level policy gate that fails when a new mutation route/message
     has no registry entry or when a public handler reaches an unapproved write or
     expensive-work class.

3. **Implement centralized trust policy**
   - Add a transport-independent `AccountTrustPolicy` under
     `backend/internal/security` with typed `Guest`, `Provisional`, `Established`,
     and `Privileged` decisions and stable denial code `account_provisional`.
   - Resolve persisted `users.created_at`, TOTP enabled state, suspension/deletion,
     and the current authenticated identity. Cache only bounded derived trust data;
     invalidate it on TOTP enable/disable and account lifecycle changes. Missing
     dependencies, database/cache inconsistency, or malformed timestamps deny
     mutations while public reads remain available.
   - Enforce the policy in domain services as the authority. HTTP middleware and
     route groups may reject early, but cannot be the only control; WebSocket and
     REST-bridge paths use the same decision API.
   - Return `403` with `reason_code`, `unlock_at`, `remaining_seconds`, and a
     server-owned TOTP setup route/action. Never expose whether another account is
     provisional.

4. **Apply the provisional mutation boundary**
   - Start with deny-by-default mutation middleware/registry, then explicitly
     allow the provisional self-service routes. A fresh registration token must
     not bypass the policy.
   - Gate forum threads/comments/likes/shares; page claims, likes, comments and
     interactive responses; inbox conversations/messages/blocks; uploads; cart,
     reviews, orders, wallet and subscriptions; documentation; provisioning; and
     all other persistent domain mutations.
   - Keep ordinary public/authenticated reads bounded and visibility-safe. Private
     reads still require their existing ownership/membership policy and are not
     granted merely by account age.
   - Add domain tests proving a provisional denial occurs before repository,
     filesystem, dispatcher, cache invalidation, payment, compiler, or WebSocket
     work, and that established/TOTP-unlocked callers still meet existing policy.

5. **Make rate limiting identity-, tier-, and cost-aware**
   - Replace the unused/local-only limiter split with Redis-backed hierarchical
     budgets keyed by tenant + IP, signed guest-session cookie, user ID, trust tier,
     action class, and resource where appropriate. An account bypass never clears
     or promotes the shared IP budget.
   - Initial configurable defaults: guest public API work is weighted within a
     60-unit/minute IP budget; registration 3/hour per IP and guest session; login
     10/15 minutes per IP plus normalized account key; recovery/verification
     3/hour; guest chat one message/15 seconds with burst one; provisional chat one
     message/8 seconds with burst one. Established chat remains configurable.
   - Give database writes, search, SSR, compiler execution, checkout/payment,
     uploads, and WebSocket fan-out higher weights than cached reads. Reject before
     database/queue work. Define and test fail-closed behavior for mutations and a
     bounded degraded cached-read behavior when Redis is unavailable.
   - Add per-IP/session WebSocket connection and upgrade budgets plus distributed
     message budgets. Per-connection token buckets remain defense in depth and
     must cover media/SFX in addition to chat, presence, cursor, and signaling.
   - Add bounded behavior isolation/penalty rooms and admin visibility only after
     deterministic tiers pass. Overrides are session/account scoped, expiring,
     fresh-step-up protected, and audited.

6. **Provide non-coercive onboarding UX**
   - After registration and on attempted restricted actions, show a persistent but
     dismissible security notice: the account can browse and use allowed chat now,
     normal account actions unlock at the server-provided time, and enabling TOTP
     unlocks them immediately.
   - Provide a live countdown driven by `/api/time`/trust-status, one clear “Set up
     TOTP” action, and a “Continue browsing” option. Do not hide or disable the
     recovery/logout paths and do not imply that TOTP is mandatory.
   - Handle stale tabs and multi-tab unlock through an authoritative user/trust
     invalidation; refetch after TOTP enablement or timer expiry. Preserve loading,
     offline, denied, and screen-reader states without client-side authorization.

7. **Audit, observe, and roll out safely**
   - Emit content-free events for tier decisions, provisional denials, unlock by
     time/TOTP, limiter denials, guest exceptions, and media/history mutations.
     Bound labels and never log message bodies, answers, credentials, or tokens.
   - Add dashboards for denial/action class, account tier, IP/session population,
     limiter failure, websocket connections/messages, and database/queue work
     prevented. Alerts distinguish abuse from shared-NAT pressure.
   - Ship immediate containment independently. Then deploy additive trust policy in
     observe-only mode, compare decisions, enable provisional enforcement for new
     registrations, and finally enable deny-by-default registry enforcement.
   - Run a two-browser matrix for guest, provisional, TOTP-unlocked provisional,
     time-unlocked established, ordinary established, owner/editor, moderator,
     admin, and changed-IP/session cases. Include reconnect, token refresh, Redis
     outage, clock boundary, concurrent requests, and direct WebSocket frames.

## Verification

- Focused negative tests assert guests cannot mutate media/history, allocate
  arbitrary media routes, invoke Grengo or receive its private telemetry, teleport,
  execute unapproved datasources, mint unapproved voice access, retrieve private
  pages/public email fields, or enumerate visitor IP/identity analytics.
- Store tests assert no public/authenticated request can mint wallet credit; only
  a verified provider/admin test path can create a credit ledger entry.
- Trust-policy table tests cover exactly-before/at/after 30 minutes, TOTP enable
  and disable, old accounts, refresh/relogin, suspended/deleted accounts, lookup
  failures, and server clock use.
- Route/message registry tests cover every mounted HTTP mutation and every
  client-originated WebSocket message exactly once.
- Distributed limiter tests cover reconnect/multi-connection bypass, shared NAT,
  multi-tenant keys, Redis failure, weighted work, penalty expiry, and override
  isolation.
- Backend: `env GOCACHE=/tmp/skaia-go-cache GOTMPDIR=/tmp JWT_SECRET=test-secret go test ./...`
- WebSocket race tests: `go test -race ./internal/ws ./internal/security`
- Frontend: relevant onboarding/auth tests, full test suite, lint, TypeScript, and
  production build.
- Manual: throttle/reconnect two browsers and verify countdown, TOTP immediate
  unlock, 30-minute unlock, guest chat feedback, and no stale privileged controls.

